<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE RESOURCE SHRINKER MAY NOT HAVE THESE.

  `isShrinkResources = true` removes every resource nothing statically
  references, and the Google Services plugin's output is referenced by NAME at
  run time and by nothing at all at compile time:

      StringResourceValueReader reader = new StringResourceValueReader(context);
      String applicationId = reader.getString("google_app_id");   // getIdentifier

  `FirebaseOptions.fromResource` returns null the moment that string is empty,
  `FirebaseApp.initializeApp` then returns null after logging one line nobody
  reads, and every later call throws

      IllegalStateException: Default FirebaseApp is not initialized in this
      process. Make sure to call FirebaseApp.initializeApp(Context) first.

  which is the fatal that arrived from a Play install on 0.2.3+441.

  THIS FILE IS INSURANCE, NOT A DIAGNOSIS. `app/build.gradle.kts` records
  measuring `google_app_id` PRESENT in resources.arsc of a shipped beta, and
  the reason it survived is that "google_app_id" appears as a string literal in
  the Firebase SDK's own dex, which the shrinker reads. That is a heuristic
  about somebody else's code holding a literal we do not control: it is one
  R8 release or one SDK refactor away from being false, and nothing would say
  so — the build would go green and push would simply stop working.

  Naming them here makes the shrinker's decision ours. It costs a few hundred
  bytes in the APK and removes a silent failure mode.

  WHAT IS NOT HERE IS `default_web_client_id`, and its absence is deliberate
  rather than an oversight. The shrinker DID strip that one, it reached people,
  and the fix was to stop needing it at run time: `app/build.gradle.kts` reads
  the value out of google-services.json and compiles it into BuildConfig, so
  there is no resource to look up. Keeping it here would resurrect a resource
  nothing reads.

  AND THIS FILE IS NOT PACKAGED. `res/raw/keep.xml` is read by AGP's shrinker
  and dropped; it does not ship. A name that matches no resource — which is
  every name below, in a fork building without google-services.json — matches
  nothing and keeps nothing.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@string/google_app_id,@string/google_api_key,@string/gcm_defaultSenderId,@string/project_id,@string/google_storage_bucket,@string/firebase_database_url" />
